  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA 1040 mb area of high pressure positioned across our region \nthis morning, will gradually shift north/east into northern New \nEngland tonight. This high will continue to provide dry \nconditions along with seasonably cool temperatures for pre-\nholiday travel today. Temperatures will start out in the 10s to \nmid 20s, rising to highs ranging from the mid 30s in the \nmountains to lower/mid 40s in lower elevations. Winds will be \nlighter today, less than 5 mph.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry conditions should persist through much of Thu A.M. before \nprecipitation develops from south to north from late Thu A.M. \ninto Thu P.M. associated with an upper-990s mb cyclone \napproaching from the N. Carolina/Virginia area. Diffluent flow \naloft and isentropic lift will result in a solid shield of \nmainly rain, occurring over a 6-10 hour time frame. An \nintensifying low-level E-SE jet will bring a significant \nincrease in deep layer moisture, with integrated water vapor \nanomalies of +1 to +2 STDEV resulting in PWAT anomalies surging \nto +2 to +3 STDEV Thu into Thu evening. Rain will fall moderate \nto heavy at times, especially during the evening. So while \nsteady/widespread rain won't last long, total QPF of around \n0.75\" to 1.75\" is expected with the greatest amounts in the E. \nCatskills into the Mid Hudson Valley and NW CT. Latest NBM \nprobabilities for > 1.00\" of rain in < 24 hours are 60-95% \nacross the region, although chances lower to 30-60% and limited \nto areas mentioned above. Given dry antecedent conditions and \nlack of rain for the past two weeks, flooding is not anticipated\nalthough ponding of water may occur especially in urban and \npoor drainage locations. Steady rain looks to taper off to \nshowers after midnight Thu night, as a dry slot moves in rather \nquickly from the south.\n\nIn terms of any wintry precip, this looks to be limited to \nmainly sheltered higher elevations (> 1500 ft) in parts of the \nS. Adirondacks and S. Greens, where surface temperatures look to\nbe at or just below 32F at the onset of precip. Latest forecast\nsoundings from RRFS indicate a fairly pronounced warm nose \naloft developing, so most precip type would likely be freezing \nrain in these limited areas (slight chance of sleet at onset). \nIsolated spots in the E. Catskills could also see a brief period\nof freezing rain, but should quickly turn over the plain rain. \nWhere the freezing rain occurs in the S. Adirondacks and S. \nGreens starting late Thu P.M., it could linger for at least a \nfew hours into the evening. Radial ice accumulation in these \nlimited areas look to be mainly a few hundredths to < 0.10\". \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAs the storm system tracks north into the mid Atlantic region \non Thu, E-SE winds will increase substantially through the day. \nNAEFs indicating easterly (u-component) 850 mb wind anomalies \nof -4 to -5 STDEV developing, with deterministic guidance \nshowing 50-70 kt. The greatest winds will likely not fully mix \ndown due to a strengthening low level inversion developing, \nhowever there may be winds of 40-50 kt displaced just beneath \nthe inversion. Strongest winds look to occur just prior to or \nduring onset of rain while the low levels are still relatively \ndry. This would be mainly late Thu afternoon into Thu evening. \nThis type of flow regime favors strongest winds in the higher \nterrain of western New England into the Taconics in E. NY. NBM \nprobabilities of > 40 mph max gusts are 60-90% in these areas, \nwith a 30-70% of > 45 mph. If confidence continues to increase \nin gusts exceeding 45 mph, a Wind Advisory would be issued. \nWinds shift to the south and start to weaken late Thu night.",
